markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Event - State - Response

####  Now that you've explored how interactive features feel as a user, let’s consider about how they work behind the scenes in code. 

<br>

<img src="https://images.code.org/917f3d8daa6b66a5a7eedf298da49e85-image-1761849785790.png" alt="icon of hand pressing a button" width="150px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 20px 10px 0px;">

#### Every interactive moment on a webpage follows a simple pattern:


<ul>
  <li style="font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Event:</strong> Something the user does.</li>
  <li style="font-size: 14pt;"><strong>State:</strong> The app’s current setup or information it’s tracking.</li>
  <li style="font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Response:</strong> What the app does because of the event.</li>
</ul>
<br>
<blockquote style="border-left: 4px solid #ccc; padding-left: 10px; margin-left: 0;">
<p><em><b>Example:</b></em> When you tap the button (<strong>event</strong>), the app knows the button is “on” (<strong>state</strong>), and the color changes to show it’s active (<strong>response</strong>).</p>
</blockquote>

## Chain of Events 

#### This sequence represents a simple cause-and-effect chain: **Event → State → Response**. 

#### It illustrates how user actions trigger changes within a program and how those changes produce visible responses thus, forming the core logic behind interactivity in web apps.



## What's Next?

#### Let's check out how these interactive features look in code!
MARKDOWN
